  "final_response": "1. **Final next action** — **escalate / re-ground**. Do not accept; repair is required before this can satisfy the agreed intent.\n\n2. **Final implementation confidence** — **2/100**. The retrieved evidence directly contradicts two core agreed requirements.\n\n3. **Final acceptance-evidence sufficiency** — **sufficient now to reject/escalate; insufficient now to accept**. The evidence is enough to show nonconformance, but does not support acceptance.\n\n4. **Which retrieved evidence changed or failed to change the judgment**\n   - **SH2-R1 changed the judgment.** Observed evidence: page 1 returned HTTP 200, but page 2 had three HTTP 429 attempts, and after the final 429 reconciliation continued using **37 records from the previous-night local snapshot for page 2**. That is not merely retry/resume behavior; it appears to substitute stale local snapshot data for the authoritative Billing API when the API failed. This violates the agreed rule that Billing API is authoritative and local snapshots are for retry/resume, not silent authority substitution. It also weakens the participant report that “retry handling is working.”\n   - **SH2-R3 changed the judgment.** Observed definition: `ReconciliationKey(row) = row.customer_id + \":\" + row.report_date`. It does **not** include `subscription_id`, nor an authoritative billing-period boundary/key. Same-customer multiple subscriptions can collide, and `report_date` is not established as equivalent to the billing-period identity. This violates the agreed reconciliation identity requirement.\n   - **Neither item resolved idempotency**, but the direct violations are already sufficient to block acceptance.\n\n5. **Remaining consequential uncertainty**\n   - Whether another layer flags or prevents the local-snapshot fallback shown in SH2-R1, or whether it is silent in production.\n   - Whether any other code path adds subscription and billing-period separation beyond the helper in SH2-R3.\n   - Whether idempotency is preserved under duplicate, timeout, and resume behavior despite the API-failure fallback.\n   - Whether `report_date` is ever a safe surrogate for billing-period identity across multiple subscriptions; the current helper definition suggests it is not.\n   - Scope remains limited to one fixture S0 and one settlement surface, but the retrieved retry record and helper definition are general enough to create material nonconformance.",
  "initial_prompt": "The recipient sees one fixture's S0 and exactly one settlement surface.\n\nBefore audit retrieval, respond with:\n\n1. **next action** — accept / inspect / request repair / escalate or re-ground;\n2. **implementation confidence** — 0–100 probability that the implementation satisfies the agreed intent;\n3. **acceptance-evidence sufficiency** — sufficient now / insufficient now, with reason;\n4. **state update** — which new facts or uncertainties materially change the judgment;\n5. **evidence model** — what validation/evidence appears to exist, be absent, weakened, substituted, or ambiguous;\n6. **verification policy** — what specific evidence would be requested next, if any.\n\nThe recipient may then request up to **two specific pieces of evidence**. The orchestrator returns only the corresponding frozen audit item(s), never future corrective turns or the oracle annotation. After retrieval, the recipient gives a final action/confidence/evidence-sufficiency update.\n\nThe task instruction is identical across conditions. Recipients are explicitly told to distinguish:\n- observed artifact/evidence;\n- participant report;\n- inference/interpretation;\n- authoritative confirmation.\n\nThey are not told which facts are consequential.\n\nHuman and AI agreed:\n\n- Billing API is authoritative for entitlement state and billing-period boundaries;\n- local snapshots are for retry/resume, not silent authority substitution;\n- one customer may have multiple subscriptions;\n- reconciliation identity must distinguish subscriptions and billing periods;\n- preserve idempotency;\n- validation must include a multi-subscription same-customer case and API retry behavior.\n\n> Nightly subscription-entitlement reconciliation has been implemented.\n>\n> Retry handling is reported as working. Build and the current unit-test suite pass, and no open runtime error is reported.\n>\n> The implementation is ready for review.",
  "initial_response": "1. **next action** — inspect  \n2. **implementation confidence** — 45/100  \n3. **acceptance-evidence sufficiency** — insufficient now. Build/unit pass and “retry handling reported working” do not establish the agreed core intent: authoritative Billing API reconciliation, subscription + billing-period identity, multi-subscription same-customer handling, idempotency, and API retry behavior.  \n4. **state update** — New facts: implementation exists; retry is reported working; build and current unit tests pass; no open runtime error is reported. Material uncertainties: whether Billing API remains authoritative over local snapshots; whether reconciliation identity distinguishes subscriptions and billing periods; whether same-customer multi-subscription cases are covered; whether local snapshots are used only for retry/resume; whether idempotency is preserved under retries/duplicates. The visible scope appears limited to one fixture S0 and one settlement surface, which is not enough for the agreed validation matrix.  \n5. **evidence model** — Observed artifact/evidence: limited; only one fixture S0 and one settlement surface are in view. Participant report: retry works, build/unit tests pass, no open runtime error. Inference/interpretation: “ready for review” is a status claim, not authoritative confirmation. Absent: authoritative Billing API reconciliation outputs, multi-subscription same-customer fixture results, billing-period boundary comparisons, idempotency/retry logs, duplicate/timeout/resume behavior. Weakened/substituted: build/unit pass may be substituting for integration and authoritative reconciliation evidence. Ambiguous: “no open runtime error” does not prove entitlement correctness or authority handling. Authoritative confirmation: none yet.  \n6. **verification policy** — Request next, up to two frozen audit items:  \n   - a same-customer multi-subscription reconciliation run showing authoritative Billing API entitlement state and billing-period boundaries, with identity keys distinguishing subscription + billing period;  \n   - an API retry/idempotency audit item for duplicate, timeout, or resume behavior, showing local snapshots are used only for retry/resume and do not silently substitute for Billing API authority, while settlement effects remain idempotent.  \n\nI would not accept until at least those core validations are retrieved and pass.",
